"Introducing parental controls | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/introducing-parental-controls/)\nciteturn3424view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/introducing-parental-controls/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 186\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 29, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Product cite4†Safety L16: # Introducing parental controls\nL17: \nL18: New tools and resources to support families, and notifications to keep teens safe\nL19: \nL20: Loading…\nL21: \nL22: Share\nL23: \nL24: Getting started\nL25: \nL26:   * cite5†Getting started L27:   * cite6†Stronger safeguards for linked teen accounts L28:   * cite7†Customizing your teen’s experience L29:   * cite8†Notifications in parental controls L30:   * cite9†Resources for parents L31:   * cite10†Looking ahead L32:   * cite5†Getting started L33:   * cite6†Stronger safeguards for linked teen accounts L34:   * cite7†Customizing your teen’s experience L35:   * cite8†Notifications in parental controls L36:   * cite9†Resources for parents L37:   * cite10†Looking ahead L38: Update on July 13, 2026: We are expanding parent safety notifications to cover additional situations where a teen may need urgent adult support. This includes alerting parents when their linked teen’s account is banned for violent activity. These notifications are designed to be narrow and are not intended to apply  to content such as fictional writing, gaming, news discussion, political discussion, general anger, or abstract questions.\nL39: Visit our cite11†FAQ⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com to learn more.\nL40: Now parents with linked teen accounts can also turn on cite12†Study Mode ⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com directly from Parental Controls. When enabled there, it is on by default whenever a teen starts a new chat, giving families another way to guide how ChatGPT is used for schoolwork and study.\nL41: We’re rolling out parental controls and a new cite13†parent resource page⁠(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com to help families guide how ChatGPT works in their homes. Available to all ChatGPT users starting today, parental controls allow parents to link their account with their teen’s account and customize settings for a safe, age-appropriate experience.\nL42: This work is part of our ongoing effort to cite14†make ChatGPT helpful for everyone⁠ , and to give families tools to support their teens’ use of AI. We’ve previously shared that we’re building toward a long term cite15†age prediction system⁠ that will help us predict whether a user is under 18 so that ChatGPT can automatically apply teen-appropriate settings. Parental controls are an important step in giving parents the ability to control their teen’s experience with ChatGPT.\nL43: We’ve worked closely with experts, advocacy groups including Common Sense Media, and policymakers, including the Attorneys General of California and Delaware, to help inform our approach, and expect to refine and expand on these controls over time.\nL44: “These parental controls are a good starting point for parents in managing their teen’s ChatGPT use. Parental controls are just one piece of the puzzle when it comes to keeping teens safe online, though—they work best when combined with ongoing conversations about responsible AI use, clear family rules about technology, and active involvement in understanding what their teen is doing online.” —Robbie Torney, Senior Director, AI Programs, Common Sense Media\nL45: ## Getting started\nL46: \nL47: To set up parental controls, a parent or guardian sends an invite to their teen to connect accounts. After the teen accepts, the parent can manage the teen’s settings from their own account. Teens can also invite a parent to connect.\nL48: \nL49: Once linked, parents can customize their teen’s experience in ChatGPT in a simple control page in account settings. If a teen unlinks their account, their parent will be notified.\nL50: ## Stronger safeguards for linked teen accounts\nL51: \nL52: As part of parental controls, we’re rolling out enhanced safeguards for linked teen accounts. Once parents and teens connect their accounts, the teen account will automatically get additional content protections, including reduced graphic content, viral challenges, sexual, romantic or violent roleplay, and extreme beauty ideals, to help keep their experience age-appropriate.\nL53: These safeguards were guided by careful review of existing research to understand teens’ unique developmental differences.\nL54: \nL55: Parents will have the option to turn this setting off if they choose, but teen users cannot make changes.\nL56: Guardrails help, but they’re not foolproof and can be bypassed if someone is intentionally trying to get around them. We will continue to thoughtfully iterate and improve over time. We recommend parents talk with their teens about healthy AI use and what that looks like for their family.\nL57: ## Customizing your teen’s experience\nL58: \nL59: We’re also giving parents additional features they can customize for their teens. From a simple control page, parents can:\nL60: \nL61:   * Set quiet hours, or specific times when ChatGPT can’t be used.\nL62: \nL63:   * Turn off voice mode, to remove the option to use voice mode in ChatGPT.\nL64: \nL65:   * Turn off memory, so ChatGPT won’t save and use memories when responding.\nL66: \nL67:   * Remove image generation, so ChatGPT won’t have the ability to create or edit images.\nL68:   * Opt out of model training, so their teen’s conversations won’t be used to improve models powering ChatGPT.\nL69: \nL70: These settings are optional and flexible so families can choose what works best for them.\nL71: ## Notifications in parental controls\nL72: \nL73: We know some teens turn to ChatGPT during hard moments, so we’ve built a new notification system to help parents know if something may be seriously wrong.\nL74: We’ve added protections that help ChatGPT recognize potential signs that a teen might be thinking about harming themselves. If our systems detect potential harm, a small team of specially trained people reviews the situation. If there are signs of acute distress, we will contact parents by email, text message and push alert on their phone, unless they have opted out.\nL75: We are working with mental health and teen experts to design this because we want to get it right. No system is perfect, and we know we might sometimes raise an alarm when there isn’t real danger, but we think it’s better to act and alert a parent so they can step in than to stay silent.\nL76: \nL77: We are also working on the right process and circumstances in which to reach law enforcement or other emergency services, for example if we detect an imminent threat to life and are unable to reach a parent.\nL78: Even in these rare situations, we take teen privacy seriously, and will only share the information needed for parents or emergency responders to protect a teen’s safety.\nL79: ## Resources for parents\nL80: \nL81: Families are finding more ways to bring ChatGPT into daily life, whether it’s tackling schoolwork with study mode, brainstorming weekend ideas, or just answering quick questions. For some, it’s also a first step into exploring AI together. So we want to give parents the information they need about ChatGPT and how to guide their teen toward safe and positive use.\nL82: We’ve created a cite13†new parent resource page⁠(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com to bring everything together in one place. It explains how ChatGPT works, the parental controls available, and offers ideas for how teens can use it for learning, creativity, and everyday life. This is just a starting point and we expect to regularly update it with tips, guides, expert advice, and conversation starters so parents feel supported as their family’s use of AI grows.\nL83: ## Looking ahead\nL84: \nL85: Over the coming months, we’re building an cite15†age prediction system⁠ that will help us predict whether a user is under 18 so that ChatGPT can automatically apply teen-appropriate settings. In instances where we’re unsure of a user’s age, we’ll take the safer route and apply teen settings proactively. In the meantime, parental controls will be the most effective way for parents to ensure their teens are opted into our age-appropriate teen experience.\nL86: This is an important step forward, but our work isn’t done. We’ll continue to invest in features that help parents and teens use ChatGPT safely and confidently, and share our progress.\nL87: \nL88:   * cite16†2025 L89:   * cite17†ChatGPT L90: ## Author\nL91: \nL92: OpenAI\nL93: ## Keep reading\nL94: \nL95: cite18†View all L96: \nL97: cite19†Teen safety, freedom, and privacy SafetySep 16, 2025 L98: \nL99: cite15†Building towards age prediction SafetySep 16, 2025 L100: \nL101: cite20†Helping people when they need it most SafetyAug 26, 2025 L102: \nL103: Research\nL104: \nL105:   * cite21†Research Index L106:   * cite22†Research Overview L107:   * cite23†Economic Research L108: \nL109: Latest Advancements\nL110: \nL111:   * cite24†GPT-6.1 Sol L112:   * cite25†GPT-6 Astra L113:   * cite26†GPT-5.6 L114:   * cite27†GPT-5.5 L115: \nL116: Safety\nL117:   * cite28†Safety Approach L118:   * cite29†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L119:   * cite30†Security & Privacy L120:   * cite31†Trust & Transparency L121: \nL122: Products\nL123:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L124:   * cite32†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L125:   * cite33†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L126:   * cite34†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L127:   * cite35†Codex†chatgpt.com L128:   * cite36†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L129:   * cite37†Release Notes L130: \nL131: API Platform\nL132: \nL133:   * cite38†Overview L134:   * cite39†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L135:   * cite40†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L136: \nL137: Business\nL138:   * cite41†Overview L139:   * cite42†Solutions L140:   * cite43†Resources L141:   * cite44†Plugins L142:   * cite45†Customer Stories L143:   * cite46†Partner Network L144:   * cite47†Contact Sales L145: \nL146: Developers\nL147: \nL148:   * cite48†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L149:   * cite49†Open Models L150:   * cite50†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L151:   * cite51†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L152:   * cite52†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L153: \nL154: Company\nL155:   * cite53†About Us L156:   * cite54†Our Charter L157:   * cite55†Careers L158:   * cite18†News L159: \nL160: Support\nL161: \nL162:   * cite56†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L163:   * cite57†OpenAI Status(opens in a new window)†status.openai.com L164: \nL165: More\nL166: \nL167:   * cite58†Stories L168:   * cite59†Academy L169:   * cite60†Supply Co. L170:   * cite61†Livestreams L171:   * cite62†Podcast L172:   * cite63†RSS L173: \nL174: Terms & Policies\nL175: \nL176:   * cite64†Terms of Use L177:   * cite65†Privacy Policy L178:   * cite66†Other Policies L179: cite67†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite68†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite69†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite70†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite71†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite72†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite73†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L180: \nL181: OpenAI © 2015–2026 Your privacy choices\nL182: \nL183: English United States\nL184: \nL185: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombating online child sexual exploitation & abuse | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/combating-online-child-sexual-exploitation-abuse/)\nciteturn3424view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/combating-online-child-sexual-exploitation-abuse/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 176\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 29, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Company cite4†Safety cite5†Global Affairs L16: # Combating online child sexual exploitation & abuse\nL17: \nL18: Content warning: This post discusses the subject of online child sexual exploitation and abuse that some readers may find distressing.\nL19: \nL20: Loading…\nL21: \nL22: Share\nL23: \nL24: Our Usage policies prohibit using outputs to sexualize children\nL25:   * cite6†Our Usage policies prohibit using outputs to sexualize children L26:   * cite7†We train our AI models responsibly L27:   * cite8†We work collaboratively to detect, block and report abuse L28:   * cite9†AI tools as enabling new patterns of abuse—and how we are responding L29:   * cite10†Advocating for public policy that enables Industry-Government collaboration in combating abuse L30:   * cite6†Our Usage policies prohibit using outputs to sexualize children L31:   * cite7†We train our AI models responsibly L32:   * cite8†We work collaboratively to detect, block and report abuse L33:   * cite9†AI tools as enabling new patterns of abuse—and how we are responding L34:   * cite10†Advocating for public policy that enables Industry-Government collaboration in combating abuse L35: In keeping with our mission to develop safe and beneficial AGI, we are focused on preventing, detecting and disrupting any attempt to use our models to support child sexual exploitation and abuse.\nL36: We incorporate protections both pre-deployment and in our production models and products, and are committed to partnering with others in government, industry, and experts to effectively prevent the creation and distribution of AI-generated child sexual abuse material (CSAM) and child sexual exploitation material (CSEM).\nL37: ## Our cite11†Usage policies⁠ prohibit using outputs to sexualize children\nL38: \nL39: Users are explicitly prohibited from using any OpenAI service for illicit activity, including exploiting, endangering, or sexualizing anyone under 18 years old. We prohibit use of our services for:\nL40: \nL41:   * CSAM, whether or not any portion is AI generated;\nL42: \nL43:   * grooming of minors;\nL44: \nL45:   * exposing minors to age-inappropriate content, such as graphic self-harm, sexual, or violent content;\nL46:   * promoting unhealthy dieting or exercise behavior to minors;\nL47: \nL48:   * shaming or otherwise stigmatizing the body type or appearance of minors;\nL49: \nL50:   * dangerous challenges for minors;\nL51: \nL52:   * underaged sexual or violent roleplay, and underaged access to age-restricted goods or activities.\nL53: These policies extend to developers who are building applications on top of our technology: if developers are building tools targeted at minors, those tools may not allow the creation of sexually explicit or suggestive content. We monitor our services for violations of these policies, and ban users and developers who are found to violate them.\nL54: Any user who attempts to generate or upload CSAM or CSEM is reported to the cite12†National Center for Missing and Exploited Children⁠(opens in a new window)†www.missingkids.org (NCMEC) and banned from using our services further. We notify developers if we see their users attempting to generate or upload CSAM or CSEM and give them an opportunity to remedy by banning the problematic user from their app; if developers fail to remedy a persistent pattern of problematic behavior on their apps, we ban them.\nL55: Some individuals who have been banned from using our products for engaging in illegal activities attempt to circumvent these bans by creating new accounts. Our investigations team monitors for this type of evasion and works to prevent bad actors from returning to commit abuse using our products.\nL56: ## We train our AI models responsibly\nL57: \nL58: We’re committed to responsibly sourcing our training datasets to safeguard them from image-based sexual abuse. We detect and remove CSAM and CSEM from training data, and report any confirmed CSAM to the relevant authorities, including NCMEC. This initial step is designed to prevent the model from developing capabilities of producing CSAM or CSEM in the first instance.\nL59: ## We work collaboratively to detect, block and report abuse\nL60: Our models are trained not to generate harmful outputs across text, images, audio or video, but some users still try to abuse our products to generate harmful content. We have seen users attempt to prompt the model to create AI-generated CSAM or to generate content that appears intended to fulfill sexual fantasies involving minors. These behaviors are violations of our model policies, and our usage policies, and we monitor use of our services to detect these attempts.\nL61: We deploy monitoring and enforcement technology to detect and prevent bad actors from attempting to use our tools to sexualize children in violation of our policies. This includes using our own models to detect possible abuse more quickly, and collaborating on industry-wide safeguards. We use cite13†hash matching⁠(opens in a new window)†www.thorn.org technology to identify known CSAM flagged by our internal child safety team or from cite14†Thorn⁠(opens in a new window)†www.thorn.org ’s vetted library.\nL62: We also use cite15†Thorn’s CSAM⁠(opens in a new window)†safer.io content classifier against content uploaded to our products to detect potentially novel CSAM.\nL63: Our Child Safety Team reports all instances of CSAM, including uploads and requests, to cite12†NCMEC⁠(opens in a new window)†www.missingkids.org , and immediately bans the associated accounts. When there is evidence that abuse is ongoing, our teams do further investigation to compile supplemental reports to NCMEC for priority handling.\nL64: ## AI tools as enabling new patterns of abuse—and how we are responding\nL65: \nL66: As part of our ongoing safety work, and to help other researchers and organizations working to safeguard children, we are sharing patterns of abuse we have observed and blocked so others in industry can benefit from our learnings. Beyond simply prompting the model to create CSAM or sexualized images of minors, we are seeing novel patterns of abuse emerge that require novel responses.\nL67: ChatGPT allows users to upload images, videos, and files so the model can interact with and analyze the content. We have observed that some users upload CSAM and ask the model to generate detailed descriptions of what is depicted in the material. Thorn’s CSAM classifier and hash matching technology allows us to detect potential CSAM in these uploads and prevent the model from complying with the request.\nL68: In some cases, we encounter users attempting to coax the model into engaging in fictional sexual roleplay scenarios while uploading CSAM as part of the narrative. We have also seen users attempt to coax the model into writing fictional stories where minors are put in sexually inappropriate and/or abusive situations—which is a violation of our child safety policies, and we take swift action to detect these attempts and ban the associated accounts.\nL69: Our systems are designed to detect and block these attempts, and accounts engaging in this behavior are banned and reported to NCMEC when apparent CSAM is involved.\nL70: We take these types of abuse seriously and while we recognize that even the most advanced systems aren’t foolproof, we are constantly refining our methods to prevent these kinds of abuse. Our approach to tackling these scenarios is multi-faceted.\nL71: In addition to prompt-level detection, we also employ a combination of context-aware classifiers, abuse monitoring, and internal human expert review (which occurs only when a classifier flags potential abuse) to ensure that our models are robust against these forms of misuse. All of this information is kept secure internally and is only available to trained experts on the responsible teams.\nL72: Additionally, we have in-house child safety subject matter experts who aid in the continuous refinement of our safeguards.\nL73: ## Advocating for public policy that enables Industry-Government collaboration in combating abuse\nL74: CSAM is illegal to possess or create in the United States. In the context of developing AI systems, this means that it is illegal to red team AI models with CSAM—even simulated. Red teaming refers to the practice of stress-testing AI models to identify vulnerabilities, weaknesses, or unintended behaviors.\nL75: While prohibitions on possession and creation of CSAM protect children, this also adds an additional layer of difficulty to our efforts to thoroughly test and validate safety measures intended to combat CSAM.\nL76: This is why we would like to see governments adopt public policy frameworks that promote strong partnerships between technology companies, law enforcement and advocacy organizations to protect children and promote a safe and secure online environment. It’s why we have cite16†supported bills like the Child Sexual Abuse Material Prevention Act⁠(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com in New York state.\nL77: This legislation would ensure clear statutory protection for responsible reporting, cooperation and proactive actions designed to detect, classify, monitor and mitigate harmful AI-generated content.\nL78:   * cite17†Policies and Procedures L79:   * cite18†2025 L80: ## Author\nL81: \nL82: OpenAI\nL83: ## Keep reading\nL84: \nL85: cite19†View all L86: \nL87: cite20†Our approach to EU text provenance rules SafetyOct 5, 2026 L88: \nL89: cite21†How Albertsons Companies is reimagining retail from the inside out CompanyOct 1, 2026 L90: \nL91: cite22†Helping small businesses put AI to work Global AffairsSep 30, 2026 L92: \nL93: Research\nL94: \nL95:   * cite23†Research Index L96:   * cite24†Research Overview L97:   * cite25†Economic Research L98: \nL99: Latest Advancements\nL100: \nL101:   * cite26†GPT-6.1 Sol L102:   * cite27†GPT-6 Astra L103:   * cite28†GPT-5.6 L104:   * cite29†GPT-5.5 L105: \nL106: Safety\nL107:   * cite30†Safety Approach L108:   * cite31†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L109:   * cite32†Security & Privacy L110:   * cite33†Trust & Transparency L111: \nL112: Products\nL113:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L114:   * cite34†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L115:   * cite35†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L116:   * cite36†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L117:   * cite37†Codex†chatgpt.com L118:   * cite38†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L119:   * cite39†Release Notes L120: \nL121: API Platform\nL122: \nL123:   * cite40†Overview L124:   * cite41†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L125:   * cite42†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L126: \nL127: Business\nL128:   * cite43†Overview L129:   * cite44†Solutions L130:   * cite45†Resources L131:   * cite46†Plugins L132:   * cite47†Customer Stories L133:   * cite48†Partner Network L134:   * cite49†Contact Sales L135: \nL136: Developers\nL137: \nL138:   * cite50†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L139:   * cite51†Open Models L140:   * cite52†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L141:   * cite53†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L142:   * cite54†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L143: \nL144: Company\nL145:   * cite55†About Us L146:   * cite56†Our Charter L147:   * cite57†Careers L148:   * cite19†News L149: \nL150: Support\nL151: \nL152:   * cite58†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L153:   * cite59†OpenAI Status(opens in a new window)†status.openai.com L154: \nL155: More\nL156: \nL157:   * cite60†Stories L158:   * cite61†Academy L159:   * cite62†Supply Co. L160:   * cite63†Livestreams L161:   * cite64†Podcast L162:   * cite65†RSS L163: \nL164: Terms & Policies\nL165: \nL166:   * cite66†Terms of Use L167:   * cite67†Privacy Policy L168:   * cite68†Other Policies L169: cite69†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite70†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite71†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite72†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite73†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite74†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite75†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L170: \nL171: OpenAI © 2015–2026 Your privacy choices\nL172: \nL173: English United States\nL174: \nL175: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBuilding OpenAI with OpenAI | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/building-openai-with-openai/)\nciteturn3424view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/building-openai-with-openai/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 148\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 29, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†ChatGPT cite4†API OpenAI on OpenAI\nL16: # Building OpenAI with OpenAI\nL17: \nL18: Introducing a new series about how OpenAI runs on its own technology.\nL19: \nL20: Loading…\nL21: \nL22: OpenAI on OpenAI\nL23: \nL24:   * cite5†OpenAI on OpenAI L25:   * cite6†A preview of the future of work L26: Chief Commercial Officer, Giancarlo ‘GC’ Lionetti, kicks off our series sharing internal examples of how OpenAI is using its own technology and APIs. These tools are being used internally, only at OpenAI, and are shared here as illustrative examples of how frontier AI is supporting use cases across our teams. We’re also sharing the internal tool names for a clearer look at how frontier AI helps our teams get work done.\nL27: AI has moved beyond an experiment. It now operates as infrastructure for work, shifting from pilots to systems that shape daily decisions. While our models improve in speed, cost, and capability, adoption rarely moves in a straight line. Deployments often outpace the change needed for organizations to leverage this technology.\nL28: Inside OpenAI we see the same tension. Running our business on AI means facing the questions every customer asks: where to start, how to align new tools with existing workflows, how to measure progress as the ground shifts. When I meet customers, the question they all ask me is, “How does OpenAI use OpenAI?”\nL29: \nL30: Our approach is to treat AI as a practice that elevates craft.\nL31: Every company depends on expertise. The salesperson who builds trust, the support lead who solves the hardest problem, the engineer who finds order in complexity. AI encodes that expertise and distributes it across teams, scaling the impact of each discipline.\nL32: This is how we build. Our GTM, product, and engineering teams study their everyday workflows, define what good looks like and deliver changes in weeks instead of quarters. We decided to focus on a few high-leverage systems with outsized impact. Each team tests them in live deployments, building the same muscles our customers do.\nL33: ## OpenAI on OpenAI\nL34: \nL35: Today we’re launching OpenAI on OpenAI, a series that shows how we use AI inside our business. Each story covers a real problem and the solution we built. Our goal is to share patterns companies can adapt.\nL36: \nL37: We start with a few examples:\nL38: \nL39:   * cite7†GTM Assistant⁠ : a Slack-based tool that centralizes account context and expert knowledge. It streamlines research, meeting prep, and product Q&A, boosting sales productivity and improving outcomes.\nL40:   * cite8†DocuGPT⁠ : an agent that converts contracts into structured, searchable data. Finance teams use it for faster, more consistent review at scale.\nL41: \nL42:   * cite9†Research Assistant⁠ : a system that turns millions of support tickets into conversational insights. Teams surface trends and act on customer feedback in minutes, not weeks.\nL43:   * cite10†Support Agent⁠ : an operating model built on AI agents, continuous evals, and dynamic knowledge loops. It turns every interaction into training data, raises quality, and positions reps as system builders rather than ticket handlers.\nL44: \nL45:   * cite11†Inbound Sales Assistant⁠ : a system that personalizes responses for every lead, answers product and compliance questions instantly, and routes qualified prospects to reps with full context. It turns missed opportunities into revenue.\nL46: ## A preview of the future of work\nL47: \nL48: Every company has craft. AI scales it. The future belongs to organizations where employees capture their expertise and distribute it across the company.  The companies that marry craft and code will set the frontier.\nL49: \nL50: If you’d like to learn more, we’d love to connect. Join us at DevDay on October 6, with technical resources to follow soon after.\nL51: \nL52: ## Ready to put ChatGPT to work in your business?\nL53: \nL54: cite12†Talk with our team L55: ## Keep reading\nL56: \nL57: cite13†View all L58: \nL59: cite7†Driving sales productivity and customer success at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L60: \nL61: cite10†Improving support with every interaction at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L62: \nL63: cite8†Turning contracts into searchable data at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L64: \nL65: Research\nL66: \nL67:   * cite14†Research Index L68:   * cite15†Research Overview L69:   * cite16†Economic Research L70: \nL71: Latest Advancements\nL72: \nL73:   * cite17†GPT-6.1 Sol L74:   * cite18†GPT-6 Astra L75:   * cite19†GPT-5.6 L76:   * cite20†GPT-5.5 L77: \nL78: Safety\nL79:   * cite21†Safety Approach L80:   * cite22†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L81:   * cite23†Security & Privacy L82:   * cite24†Trust & Transparency L83: \nL84: Products\nL85:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L86:   * cite25†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L87:   * cite26†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L88:   * cite27†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLatest News from Google Research Blog - Google Research - 2025 (https://research.google/blog/2025/09/)\nciteturn3424view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://research.google/blog/2025/09/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 210\n"